statement.before:
For a given value of the Jacobi constant $C_J$, the particle can only be where $2\Omega(x, y, z) \ge C_J$, since $v^2 = 2\Omega - C_J \ge 0$. The boundary $2\Omega = C_J$ is the **zero-velocity curve** (surface in 3D); the allowed sets are the **Hill regions**. In decreasing order of $C_J$, with $C_{L1} > C_{L2} > C_{L3} > C_{L4} = C_{L5}$:
- $C_J > C_{L1}$: three disjoint regions — around the primary, around the secondary, and far outside; a particle near one mass is trapped there forever;
- $C_{L2} < C_J < C_{L1}$: a neck opens at $L_1$ and the two inner regions merge;
- $C_{L3} < C_J < C_{L2}$: a second neck opens at $L_2$, so escape to infinity becomes possible past the secondary;
- $C_{L4} < C_J < C_{L3}$: the neck at $L_3$ opens and the forbidden zones shrink to two tadpoles around $L_4$, $L_5$;
- $C_J < C_{L4}$: no planar region is forbidden.

statement.after:
For a given value of the Jacobi constant $C_J$, the particle can only be where $2\Omega(x, y, z) \ge C_J$, since $v^2 = 2\Omega - C_J \ge 0$. The boundary $2\Omega = C_J$ is the **zero-velocity curve** (surface in 3D); the allowed sets are the **Hill regions**. For the ordered planar list below assume $0<\mu<1/2$. In decreasing order of $C_J$, with $C_{L1} > C_{L2} > C_{L3} > C_{L4} = C_{L5}$:
- $C_J > C_{L1}$: three disjoint regions — around the primary, around the secondary, and far outside; a collision-free trajectory near one mass stays in that component for as long as its solution exists;
- $C_{L2} < C_J < C_{L1}$: a neck opens at $L_1$ and the two inner regions merge;
- $C_{L3} < C_J < C_{L2}$: a second neck opens at $L_2$, so escape to infinity becomes possible past the secondary;
- $C_{L4} < C_J < C_{L3}$: the neck at $L_3$ opens and the forbidden zones shrink to two tadpoles around $L_4$, $L_5$;
- $C_J < C_{L4}$: no planar region is forbidden.
